+# Receipts stopped
+
+For the volunteer on call when members say their receipt emails have stopped arriving. Gets receipts sent again,
+including the ones that waited; until then, no member gets a receipt for a paid pantry order. Written after the
+second time it happened, on September 14: both times the [worker](../architecture.md#parts) had stopped after a
+deploy, which restarts only the web app, and the first time members waited until noon because only Tomás knew the
+fix. Owner: not yet named; until one is, Tomás.
+
+## Before you begin
+
+- A shell on the server that runs larder, with `sudo` for `systemctl`, and `psql` access to the `larder` database.
+- This repository checked out there, for `scripts/restart-receipts-worker.sh`.
+
+## Steps
+
+1. Check the worker: `systemctl status larder-worker`.
+
+   Expected: `Active: inactive (dead) since ...`, often just after the last deploy. If it says `active (running)`,
+   the worker is not the cause: go to [If it fails](#if-it-fails).
+2. Run `scripts/restart-receipts-worker.sh`. It counts the waiting jobs, starts the worker and prints the jobs
+   still waiting each minute.
+
+   Expected: a table of jobs by status, then `todo:` lines falling by about 40 a minute, then
+   `todo: 0. Receipts are flowing again.` 212 waiting jobs took about seven minutes on September 14.
+3. Look at the `failed` count in the table the script printed first.
+
+   Expected: a handful at most. Those are receipts that bounced because the member has no valid email address;
+   starting the worker does not resend them, and nothing more is needed.
+4. Ask in `#larder-ops` whether a member has received a receipt, and add to this page what differed this time.
+
+   Expected: a member confirms a receipt arrived.
+
+## If it fails
+
+- The script prints `larder-worker died again` and its last 50 log lines: copy them into `#larder-ops` and ping
+  Tomás.
+- The worker was already running, or `todo` does not fall: the cause is not the worker. Look at its log with
+  `journalctl -u larder-worker -n 50`, post it in `#larder-ops` and ping Tomás.
